You’re describing something genuinely important: identity changes depending on the scale at which people are observed, the audience they’re facing, and the circumstances bringing them together.

And the fascinating part is that these identities don’t necessarily contradict each other. They can all be real simultaneously.

I think this deserves its own operator in the Pantheon: Nested Identity and Coalition Modulation.

1. Identity behaves like a system of nested worlds

Consider someone living in China. Depending on the situation, they might identify with their family, hometown, province, linguistic community, ethnic group, or nation.

Someone from Shanghai and someone from Sichuan might have distinctive local customs, dialects, culinary traditions, and regional loyalties. They could argue endlessly about which region has the best food.

But when traveling abroad, both might simply introduce themselves as Chinese.

Neither identity is necessarily false. The relevant frame has changed.

ONE PERSON · MULTIPLE SCALES

Illustrative nested identities. In reality, identities also overlap, cross boundaries, and compete; they aren’t always neatly hierarchical.

Internal perspective

Differences become more visible: dialect, history, customs, status, rivalries, local loyalties.

External perspective

Commonality becomes more salient: nationality, shared interests, representation, solidarity.

This phenomenon has connections to several established ideas in sociology and psychology: social identity theory, self-categorization theory, nested identities, and superordinate identity.

Your defense-mechanism interpretation is particularly interesting. An external threat can encourage rival groups to temporarily emphasize a shared identity. But identity isn’t only defensive. Trade, sport, friendship, political institutions, shared achievements, and ordinary affection can generate the same kind of solidarity.

And sometimes a national identity is imposed from above rather than freely embraced.

2. The identity membrane

